DOM events propagate in **three phases**: **capturing** (window → target), **target**, then **bubbling** (target → window). Most handlers use bubbling, but understanding both phases is key for delegation and stopping propagation correctly.

## Propagation phases

```
Capturing:  window → document → ... → parent → target
Target:     event fires on target
Bubbling:   target → parent → ... → document → window
```

```javascript
document.getElementById("parent").addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("parent bubble");
});

document.getElementById("parent").addEventListener(
  "click",
  () => {
    console.log("parent capture");
  },
  true,
); // useCapture = true

document.getElementById("child").addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("child target");
});

// Click child → parent capture → child target → parent bubble
```

## Stopping propagation

| Method | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `event.stopPropagation()` | Stops further propagation on current phase |
| `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` | Also blocks other listeners on same element/phase |
| `event.preventDefault()` | Prevents default browser action (not same as stop) |

## Event delegation

Attach one listener on a parent; use `event.target` to handle children — fewer listeners, works for dynamic children.

```javascript
list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  const item = e.target.closest("[data-id]");
  if (!item || !list.contains(item)) return;
  handleSelect(item.dataset.id);
});
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "Events capture from root to target, then bubble back. Default listeners are bubbling. I use delegation on parents for lists. stopPropagation halts the chain; preventDefault stops default behavior like link navigation."

## Common mistakes

- Confusing `preventDefault` with `stopPropagation`
- Forgetting third argument `true` for capture listeners
- Delegation without checking `closest` / `contains` (clicks on nested elements)

## Key takeaways

- Three phases: capture → target → bubble.
- Delegation = one parent listener + `event.target` / `closest`.
- `stopPropagation` ≠ `preventDefault`.

## Interview follow-ups

- **React synthetic events?** — Delegated to root; `e.stopPropagation()` still works; pooling removed in React 17+.
- **once option?** — `{ once: true }` auto-removes listener after first fire.

**Layout thrashing** is when you force the browser to recalculate layout many times in a single frame, by interleaving DOM reads and writes. It turns an O(n) loop into O(n) *layouts*, and it is the most common cause of jank that a profiler will actually let you fix.

To put it simply: the browser wants to batch your layout work, and reading a geometry property at the wrong moment forces it to stop and do the work immediately instead.

## The Problem

The browser is already smart about this. When you write to the DOM, it doesn't recalculate layout right away — it marks the document dirty and queues the work to be flushed once, before the next paint. Batching is the default and it's efficient.

But some properties can only be answered with **current, accurate** geometry. If you ask for one while there are pending writes, the browser has no choice: it must flush the layout *right now*, synchronously, before it can return a number.

That's a **forced synchronous layout**. One is fine. In a loop, it's a disaster:

```javascript
// Every iteration: write (dirty) → read (forced layout). N layouts.
for (const box of boxes) {
  box.style.width = box.offsetWidth + 10 + 'px';
}
```

With 100 elements that's 100 full layout passes in one frame. At 60fps you have 16.7ms per frame total, and you have just spent all of it.

## The Solution

**Batch all reads, then all writes.**

```javascript
// Read phase — one layout flush for the whole loop
const widths = boxes.map((box) => box.offsetWidth);

// Write phase — nothing forces a flush, so it happens once at the end
boxes.forEach((box, i) => {
  box.style.width = widths[i] + 10 + 'px';
});
```

Same work, same result. **One layout instead of one hundred.**

This read-then-write discipline has a name — the **read/write batching** or "FastDOM" pattern — and stating it by name is a good signal.

## What actually triggers a forced layout?

Anything that needs up-to-date geometry. The list worth remembering:

| Category | Properties |
| --- | --- |
| **Element geometry** | `offsetWidth/Height/Top/Left`, `clientWidth/Height`, `scrollWidth/Height`, `scrollTop/Left` |
| **Methods** | `getBoundingClientRect()`, `getClientRects()`, `scrollIntoView()`, `focus()` |
| **Computed style** | `getComputedStyle(el)` — for any property that depends on layout |
| **Window** | `window.innerWidth/Height`, `scrollX/Y` |

Reading these is *not* inherently bad. Reading them **after a write, inside a loop** is.

## In React specifically

React batches its own state updates, so this rarely bites you through normal rendering. It bites you in **refs and effects**:

```javascript
// Runs before paint — a read here is a forced synchronous layout,
// but it's the correct place if you need to measure and adjust
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const height = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().height;
  setTooltipPosition(height);   // adjust before the user sees anything
}, []);
```

`useLayoutEffect` runs **synchronously after DOM mutation but before paint** — which is exactly why it's the right tool for measure-then-adjust, and exactly why overusing it blocks painting. `useEffect` runs *after* paint, so measuring there causes a visible flicker as the element jumps to its corrected position ([/react-guide/8](/react-guide/8)).

The other React-flavored version is a scroll or resize handler that reads geometry on every event. Those fire far more often than once per frame — so throttle them, or better, use `requestAnimationFrame` so they run at most once per frame:

```javascript
let scheduled = false;

window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (scheduled) return;
  scheduled = true;

  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    const y = window.scrollY;   // read once per frame, not per event
    update(y);
    scheduled = false;
  });
});
```

Better still for visibility checks: **`IntersectionObserver`**, which reports element visibility without you reading geometry at all. If you're asked how to implement infinite scroll or lazy loading, that's the answer that shows current knowledge.

## How to spot it

Chrome DevTools → Performance → record. Look for:

- Long purple **Layout** blocks
- The warning **"Forced reflow is a likely performance bottleneck"**
- A sawtooth pattern of many small layout bars inside one frame

That warning names the offending line. It's one of the most directly actionable things the profiler tells you.

## Trade-offs and follow-ups

**"Isn't this premature optimization?"** — For ten elements, yes. It matters when you're touching many elements per frame, or in a scroll handler. The honest answer is: write it naturally, profile, and fix it when the profiler points there.

**"What about `will-change`?"** — It promotes an element to its own compositor layer so transforms skip layout and paint entirely. But each layer costs GPU memory, so applying it broadly makes things *worse*. Add it just before an animation and remove it after.

**"How do you animate without touching layout at all?"** — Animate only `transform` and `opacity`. Those run on the compositor thread and skip layout and paint completely ([/css/6](/css/6)). Animating `left` or `width` triggers layout on every single frame.

## What is AJAX?

**AJAX** stands for **Asynchronous JavaScript and XML**. It's a set of technologies that enable sending/receiving data from a server **without reloading the page**.

## Technologies involved

| Technology | Role |
|------------|------|
| **HTML** | Web page structure |
| **CSS** | Styling |
| **JavaScript** | Behavior, DOM updates |
| **XMLHttpRequest API** | Send and retrieve data |
| **Server-side language** | PHP, Python, Node.js, etc. |

## Modern AJAX

Today, `XMLHttpRequest` is largely replaced by the `fetch` API:

```javascript
// Old way
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open("GET", "/api/data");
xhr.onload = () => {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(JSON.parse(xhr.responseText));
  }
};
xhr.send();

// Modern way
fetch("/api/data")
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then((data) => console.log(data));
```

Web Workers allow you to run JavaScript in **background threads**, enabling concurrent execution without blocking the main UI thread.

## Key Limitations

- Web Workers do not have access to the **DOM**, the `window` object, or the `document` object.
- They communicate via **structured cloning** — you can't pass functions or certain complex class instances, only data.

## Basic usage

```javascript
// main thread
const worker = new Worker("worker.js");
worker.postMessage({ task: "compute", data: largeArray });
worker.onmessage = (e) => console.log("Result:", e.data);

// worker.js
self.onmessage = (e) => {
  const result = heavyComputation(e.data.data);
  self.postMessage(result);
};
```

## When to use

- CPU-intensive computations (image processing, data parsing)
- Background sync or prefetching
- Anything that would block the main thread and hurt UI responsiveness
